WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:13.460
If you're publishing in the scientific literature,
you need to be aware of the problem of predatory

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.520
open-access journals.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.630
Predatory open-access journals are bogus journals
that are exploiting the open-access model

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:22.000
to make money.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.570
Open-access journals are a great idea.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:29.060
The idea is that authors pay publishing fees
so that journals can make the full text of

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.119
articles freely available to everyone.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:37.910
The PLOS journals, Public Library of Science
journals, are shining examples of this open-access

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:39.970
publishing model.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.809
When it's done with integrity, open-access
publishing is fantastic.

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:48.809
But, because money is exchanging hands, this
has opened the floodgates for the creation

00:00:48.809 --> 00:00:54.260
of bogus journals which exist solely to collect
money from authors, not to publish high-quality

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.570
research.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:02.639
Jeffrey Beall, a librarian at the University
of Colorado, Denver, has been a leader in

00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:09.930
calling out this practice and keeping lists
of bad publishers and bad journals.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:15.040
Another leader in exposing this whole problem
is the journalist John Bohannon.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.290
He ran a sort of sting operation that he wrote
about in Science in 2013.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.440
What he did was he submitted an obviously
flawed bogus paper.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:28.820
He describes it as he did a scientific version
of Mad Libs so that he could come up with

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:34.060
255 slightly different versions of the same
paper where he took molecule X from lichen

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.730
species Y inhibits the growth of cancer cell
Z.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:42.140
He created a whole bunch of different papers
just substituting different molecules and

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.790
species and cancer cells.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.640
And the papers had obvious flaws.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.860
Any scientist, no matter how schooled, should
have been able to look at figure 1 and know

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.550
that this paper was problematic.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:58.930
He submitted this to open-access journals,
many of which he suspected to be predatory

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.590
journals.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:07.620
And indeed, of 255 where he got a decision
back, 157 of those journals accepted that

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.620
paper.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:11.720
It was only rejected by 98.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:17.480
In fact, 82 percent of the publishers on Jeffrey
Beall's list actually accepted that paper.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:22.220
So, John Bohannan was able to show that there
is this huge suite out there of predatory

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:28.360
journals and they are willing to accept anything
for money.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.090
Along with the report, Science published a
map that shows the location of publishers,

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:38.470
editors, and their bank accounts, color-coded
by acceptance or rejection of the paper.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:43.500
The locations were derived from IP address
traces within the raw headers of e-mails,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:48.920
WHOIS registrations, and bank invoices for
publication fees.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.960
India emerged as the world's largest base
for fee-charging open-access publishing (64

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:58.380
publishers), and over 90% of them accepted
the paper.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:03.160
The United States is the next largest base,
with 29 publishers accepting the paper and

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.530
26 rejecting it.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:13.830
In Africa, Nigeria has the largest number,
of which 100% accepted the paper.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:18.500
All evidence shows us that these predatory
journals are greatly on the rise.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:26.310
There was a 2015 paper in BMC Medicine that
tracked predatory journals from 2010 to 2014.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:29.130
You can see it's growing exponentially.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:32.280
Pay attention to that red line on top.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.310
That's the total number of predatory journals.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:40.790
They estimated that in 2014, there were about
8,000 active predatory journals.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:46.680
In 2010, these journals published about 53,000
articles.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:53.480
That was up to 420,000 articles published
in these predatory journals in 2014.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.060
These are probably all or most of poor quality.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:03.430
Authors paid an average article processing
charge of US$178 per article, so it's a money-making

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:04.890
scam.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.240
That same paper, they also share that this
affects all scientific disciplines.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.400
You can see in this graph here that no scientific
discipline was safe from this practice.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:17.709
In 2013, Declan Butler this checklist in Nature
to help you vet journals, to make sure that

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:22.049
you're being careful to not submit to one
of these predatory journals.

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:26.889
He suggested that author should check that
the publisher provides full, verifiable contact

00:04:26.889 --> 00:04:28.470
information.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:32.100
You want the full address of the publisher
to be available.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:36.140
You also want to check that the journal's
editorial board lists actual experts with

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:39.590
full affiliations, not just made-up people.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:43.630
You might even want to contact them and ask
them about their experience with the publisher

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:47.199
because there are known instances when people's
names have been borrowed and just stuck on

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:50.440
these websites without the person's permission.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:56.010
You want to check that the journal prominently
displays its policy for author fees.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:59.760
Open-access journals do charge fees, but you
want to make sure that these policies are

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.710
clearly described on their website.

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:05.590
If they're not, that's a suspicious sign.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.780
Be wary of spam e-mail invitations.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:12.770
You will get a lot of these e-mails that ask
you to submit to journals or to become an

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:16.580
editorial board member or to speak at a conference.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:24.010
You can tell that these are spam if you look
carefully.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:28.180
Always go to the journal's website and make
sure that the articles that they are publishing

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.730
are high-quality articles.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:35.010
Check that a journal's peer-review process
is clearly described.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:39.170
Make sure that they are actually doing peer
review, and try to find out whether a journal's

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:42.230
claimed impact factor is correct.

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:47.060
There is a trend now where these predatory
journals are making up bogus metrics.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:52.199
They have names like the universal impact
factor, UIF, or the global impact factor,

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:56.290
GIF, or the journal impact factor, JIF.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:00.920
These are all popular but bogus metrics made
up by predatory journals.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:05.850
They sound a lot like the real thing, so be
careful not to get sucked into that.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:10.860
There are lists people publish of supposedly
legitimate open-access journals.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.759
The lists are not perfect, but at least the
journals listed there have gone through some

00:06:14.759 --> 00:06:16.080
vetting.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.040
You might want to check before submitting,
you can check with the Directory of Open Access

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:24.620
Journals or the Open Access Scholarly Publishers
Association.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:28.820
You can check there and make sure that the
journal you're submitting to is listed there.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.850
Finally, use common sense.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:36.270
If something looks fishy, if it looks like
spam, it probably is.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.180
So just be careful and be aware of these predatory
journals.

